    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d905">

                <pb facs="1185" n="1175" xml:id="pg1175"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">125.3516/6–1746: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Smith</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-06-17T19:00:00+03:00">June 17, 1946—7
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 18—9:04 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1908. Only two messages from Dairen have been received in Moscow. One came
                    through FonOff, was subject of my telegram 1325, April 25; second, through
                    commercial channels, reported in Embtel 1563, May 18. I spoke to Lozovski<anchor
                        n="27" xml:id="d905-f1"/> on June 9 (ReEmbtel 1850, June 10<anchor n="28"
                        xml:id="d905-f2"/>) in regard communication facilities for Benninghoff at
                    Dairen and I confirmed in letter to him on June 10 our request for arrangements
                    to be made to provide communication facilities for Dairen, at same time
                    requesting him to confirm receipt by Benninghoff of our message transmitted
                    through FonOff on April 25. Lozovski says Moscow communications with Dairen are
                    very sketchy and I am inclined to believe him.</p>
                <p>This is in reply to Deptel 1975 [<hi rendition="#italic">1075</hi>], June
                        12.<anchor n="29" xml:id="d905-f3"/> Dept please repeat to Nanking.<anchor
                        n="30" xml:id="d905-f4"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Smith</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d905-f1" n="27" xml:id="d905-f1-content">
                <p>Solomon Abramovich Lozovsky, Soviet Deputy Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d905-f2" n="28" xml:id="d905-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d905-f3" n="29" xml:id="d905-f3-content">
                <p>Not printed; it repeated telegram No. 1, June 6, 5 p.m., from the Consul General
                    at Dairen, <hi rendition="#italic">supra</hi>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d905-f4" n="30" xml:id="d905-f4-content">
                <p>Repeated in Department’s unnumbered telegram of June 18, noon.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
